WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.940
Welcome to the Federal Modernization
Workbook's developer workbook,

2
00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:06.287
Building the Attribute-Driven Migration.

3
00:00:06.287 --> 00:00:11.887
This is Chapter 1: the middleware between
the H R extract and Microsoft Entra I D.

4
00:00:11.887 --> 00:00:15.619
The provisioning service will do
whatever the payload tells it,

5
00:00:15.619 --> 00:00:19.562
so the code that builds the payload
is where the H R record stays true.

6
00:00:19.562 --> 00:00:23.072
We'll start from Microsoft's
own sample, C S V to SCIM,

7
00:00:23.072 --> 00:00:28.637
see what it leaves to you, then walk the
hardened client, two labs and the dangers.

8
00:00:28.637 --> 00:00:34.140
One note before we begin: the code is
illustrative, tested against fixtures.

9
00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:39.990
Why does this chapter matter so much?

10
00:00:39.990 --> 00:00:42.765
Because the provisioning
service is obedient.

11
00:00:42.765 --> 00:00:46.975
It owns the mapping to directory
attributes, the matching and the write,

12
00:00:46.975 --> 00:00:51.327
and Microsoft deliberately keeps the
comparison logic out of the client.

13
00:00:51.327 --> 00:00:54.850
That means everything before
the post belongs to you:

14
00:00:54.850 --> 00:00:58.853
what gets sent, in what order, how
fast, and what record you keep.

15
00:00:58.853 --> 00:01:01.152
And the endpoint is asynchronous.

16
00:01:01.152 --> 00:01:04.457
An accepted response only
means the batch was staged,

17
00:01:04.457 --> 00:01:09.189
not that anyone was written, so reading
the logs afterwards is part of the job.

18
00:01:09.189 --> 00:01:10.970
Next, which cloud.

19
00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:18.453
Which cloud you're in decides which
Graph host you call and where your

20
00:01:18.453 --> 00:01:22.382
token comes from, and a token from one
cloud simply doesn't work in another.

21
00:01:22.382 --> 00:01:26.303
A G C C tenant like this estate's
uses the worldwide endpoint,

22
00:01:26.303 --> 00:01:29.183
so Microsoft's sample
works here as written.

23
00:01:29.183 --> 00:01:32.017
The higher government clouds
have their own hosts,

24
00:01:32.017 --> 00:01:35.257
and the sample's address is
hard-coded, so it can't reach them.

25
00:01:35.257 --> 00:01:38.447
The hardened client takes
its endpoint from settings

26
00:01:38.447 --> 00:01:41.069
and only accepts one of
the three Graph hosts.

27
00:01:41.069 --> 00:01:44.010
Now let's see what Microsoft
actually gives you.

28
00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:50.072
Here's the whole pipeline on one slide.

29
00:01:50.072 --> 00:01:53.760
An H R extract comes in as a C S V file.

30
00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:58.179
The middleware validates it, normalizes
it, orders it, batches it and

31
00:01:58.179 --> 00:02:02.835
paces the calls, then posts bulk
requests to the provisioning endpoint.

32
00:02:02.835 --> 00:02:04.885
Along the way it writes four files.

33
00:02:04.885 --> 00:02:09.910
Three of them carry only keys and hashes,
so they can live in the evidence store.

34
00:02:09.910 --> 00:02:13.954
The quarantine file holds the
refused rows, real personal data,

35
00:02:13.954 --> 00:02:17.298
so it goes only to the
access-controlled audit store.

36
00:02:17.298 --> 00:02:21.750
Keep those four files in
mind; the labs read every one.

37
00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:29.699
Microsoft publishes a PowerShell
sample for exactly this integration,

38
00:02:29.699 --> 00:02:31.938
and Learn walks through it step by step.

39
00:02:31.938 --> 00:02:33.337
It has five modes.

40
00:02:33.337 --> 00:02:38.538
Two run entirely offline: checking your
mapping file and generating the payload.

41
00:02:38.538 --> 00:02:43.204
The other three talk to Graph: sending,
extending the provisioning app's schema,

42
00:02:43.204 --> 00:02:46.363
and summarizing what happened
in recent sync cycles.

43
00:02:46.363 --> 00:02:49.138
The chapter's advice is simple: use it.

44
00:02:49.138 --> 00:02:52.672
Most of what a first integration
needs is already there,

45
00:02:52.672 --> 00:02:56.513
maintained by Microsoft, with
a Learn page behind every step.

46
00:02:56.513 --> 00:02:59.180
Next, the idea at its center.

47
00:03:02.930 --> 00:03:05.680
The sample's main idea
is its mapping file.

48
00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:10.303
Each SCIM attribute names a column in
your extract, or a tiny script that

49
00:03:10.303 --> 00:03:15.005
computes the value, like turning a
worker status into active or inactive.

50
00:03:15.005 --> 00:03:18.240
Learn is careful to say this
only maps the file to SCIM;

51
00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:21.917
mapping SCIM to the directory
stays in the provisioning app.

52
00:03:21.917 --> 00:03:25.813
And because a link to a sample's
main branch changes under you,

53
00:03:25.813 --> 00:03:30.955
the repository keeps an unmodified, hashed
copy, with a weekly watch on upstream.

54
00:03:30.955 --> 00:03:35.480
A new version becomes a decision
someone reviews, never a surprise.

55
00:03:39.230 --> 00:03:40.992
This is the heart of the chapter.

56
00:03:40.992 --> 00:03:46.042
Run Microsoft's sample on the book's
nineteen-row fixture and it sends all nineteen.

57
00:03:46.042 --> 00:03:50.829
That's not a bug; it's a converter, and
Learn says validation is something you add.

58
00:03:50.829 --> 00:03:55.501
But look at what gets through: a blank
key, a pending worker sent as active,

59
00:03:55.501 --> 00:03:59.855
the same person twice, a future hire
who could sign in before day one.

60
00:03:59.855 --> 00:04:04.720
The hardened client refuses nine of those
rows, sends leavers first and managers

61
00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:09.005
before their reports, and holds a run
that would disable too many people.

62
00:04:09.005 --> 00:04:11.380
There's a second half to this table.

63
00:04:15.130 --> 00:04:17.668
The second half is
about running it safely.

64
00:04:17.668 --> 00:04:22.286
The sample has no pacing of its own, it
only reaches the worldwide endpoint,

65
00:04:22.286 --> 00:04:26.829
and its sign-in asks for a permission
that can change any app in the tenant.

66
00:04:26.829 --> 00:04:30.504
It also gives every operation
a fresh random identifier,

67
00:04:30.504 --> 00:04:35.742
so the same input never produces the same
batch, and its only record is the console.

68
00:04:35.742 --> 00:04:39.371
The verdict is balanced: keep
using the sample where it's strong,

69
00:04:39.371 --> 00:04:42.929
for checking mappings, extending
the schema and reading logs,

70
00:04:42.929 --> 00:04:46.068
and send production data
through the hardened client.

71
00:04:46.068 --> 00:04:48.690
Or contribute these changes upstream.

72
00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:53.978
So what does the example add?

73
00:04:53.978 --> 00:04:58.211
Two clients, one in Python and one
in PowerShell, that read the same

74
00:04:58.211 --> 00:05:02.602
mapping file and produce exactly
the same output for the same input.

75
00:05:02.602 --> 00:05:05.915
The Python one needs nothing
beyond the standard library.

76
00:05:05.915 --> 00:05:09.044
One mapping file holds the
column map, the code lists,

77
00:05:09.044 --> 00:05:12.902
the worker types and the length
limits, and a settings file holds

78
00:05:12.902 --> 00:05:16.977
the non-secret knobs, like the
disable threshold and the call budget.

79
00:05:16.977 --> 00:05:20.377
The payload itself keeps the
shape of Learn's own examples.

80
00:05:20.377 --> 00:05:22.550
Let's look at the rules it applies.

81
00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:31.063
Validation is where a bad H R row
stops before it becomes a bad account.

82
00:05:31.063 --> 00:05:35.638
Each row either becomes a SCIM operation
or is refused with reasons and goes

83
00:05:35.638 --> 00:05:40.287
to quarantine, along with the unchanged
row and the payload it would have sent.

84
00:05:40.287 --> 00:05:44.002
Some things are only warnings:
someone on leave isn't a leaver,

85
00:05:44.002 --> 00:05:46.899
and a departing manager
never blocks a new hire.

86
00:05:46.899 --> 00:05:50.494
The chapter's rule of thumb is
that every new check starts

87
00:05:50.494 --> 00:05:54.012
as a refusal until the H R owner
agrees it can be a warning.

88
00:05:54.012 --> 00:05:56.844
And the client always
sends the complete record,

89
00:05:56.844 --> 00:06:01.300
because with clearing switched on,
a missing value can wipe the target.

90
00:06:05.050 --> 00:06:07.350
Next, the order and the speed.

91
00:06:07.350 --> 00:06:11.595
Leavers go first, so a departing
mover never briefly gains access,

92
00:06:11.595 --> 00:06:15.762
and managers go before their
reports so the references resolve.

93
00:06:15.762 --> 00:06:20.672
Two guards hold the whole run until a
person confirms it: too many disables, or

94
00:06:20.672 --> 00:06:26.049
more calls than the daily budget, which
follows Microsoft's tenant quota by license.

95
00:06:26.049 --> 00:06:29.331
On speed, two Learn pages
disagree on the rate limit,

96
00:06:29.331 --> 00:06:33.394
so the client paces to the stricter
one, honors the retry header,

97
00:06:33.394 --> 00:06:37.300
and stops at the first failure
rather than retrying in a loop.

98
00:06:37.300 --> 00:06:39.640
Next, the record it keeps.

99
00:06:43.390 --> 00:06:45.815
This file is what an
auditor will thank you for.

100
00:06:45.815 --> 00:06:50.948
It ties every batch to the exact extract
and mapping that produced it, by hash,

101
00:06:50.948 --> 00:06:55.103
so you can walk from a line in
the provisioning logs to a person,

102
00:06:55.103 --> 00:06:56.977
to a batch, to the input files.

103
00:06:56.977 --> 00:06:59.661
That only works because each operation's

104
00:06:59.661 --> 00:07:02.740
identifier is the person
key, not a random value.

105
00:07:02.740 --> 00:07:06.953
It holds keys and hashes only, so
it can live in the evidence store.

106
00:07:06.953 --> 00:07:11.442
Both clients pass the same tests, and
seven of them pin the sample's behavior,

107
00:07:11.442 --> 00:07:15.990
so if Microsoft improves it, you'll
know it's time to shrink your own code.

108
00:07:19.740 --> 00:07:20.802
Who is this for?

109
00:07:20.802 --> 00:07:25.103
Developers who own the H R integration,
comfortable in PowerShell or

110
00:07:25.103 --> 00:07:29.478
Python and familiar with SCIM, but they
don't need to know the H R product.

111
00:07:29.478 --> 00:07:34.289
The engineers who review it, who should be
able to say where every rule came from.

112
00:07:34.289 --> 00:07:38.178
And the security officer, who
needs records rather than a screen.

113
00:07:38.178 --> 00:07:40.566
The permissions are the other half of who.

114
00:07:40.566 --> 00:07:43.877
The sender gets one upload
permission and nothing else;

115
00:07:43.877 --> 00:07:46.253
reading the logs is a separate identity.

116
00:07:46.253 --> 00:07:49.944
And note that two Learn pages
name different log permissions,

117
00:07:49.944 --> 00:07:53.210
so try the narrower one
first in your test tenant.

118
00:07:56.960 --> 00:08:00.797
Lab 1 runs on a laptop with
no tenant and no network.

119
00:08:00.797 --> 00:08:03.561
First you run Microsoft's
sample on the fixture.

120
00:08:03.561 --> 00:08:07.960
The mapping checks out, and the
payload holds all nineteen operations.

121
00:08:07.960 --> 00:08:10.918
Then go looking: the
pending worker is active,

122
00:08:10.918 --> 00:08:14.835
one person appears twice, and
one operation has an empty key.

123
00:08:14.835 --> 00:08:17.848
Next you run the hardened
client on the same file.

124
00:08:17.848 --> 00:08:21.843
It exits with a code that says
rows were refused: nine ready,

125
00:08:21.843 --> 00:08:27.086
nine quarantined, one superseded, and
the PowerShell client agrees exactly.

126
00:08:27.086 --> 00:08:28.750
Now compare the two.

127
00:08:32.500 --> 00:08:35.300
Now the comparison makes
the gap table real.

128
00:08:35.300 --> 00:08:38.387
The pending worker sits in
quarantine with its reason.

129
00:08:38.387 --> 00:08:43.146
Our first operation is the leaver, while
the sample starts at the top of the file,

130
00:08:43.146 --> 00:08:47.025
and the future hire is disabled in
ours but active in the sample's.

131
00:08:47.025 --> 00:08:50.606
Hash the extract yourself
and it matches the manifest;

132
00:08:50.606 --> 00:08:54.425
run each tool twice and only
ours gives identical batches.

133
00:08:54.425 --> 00:08:58.914
Then break things on purpose: a
lower-case department code is refused,

134
00:08:58.914 --> 00:09:01.513
and a strict disable limit holds the run.

135
00:09:01.513 --> 00:09:05.270
Finish with the tests,
forty-eight in each language.

136
00:09:09.020 --> 00:09:13.495
Lab 2 moves to a test tenant with
fictional records, never production.

137
00:09:13.495 --> 00:09:17.827
You create the provisioning app, extend
its schema with Microsoft's sample,

138
00:09:17.827 --> 00:09:20.761
and give the sender one
upload permission through

139
00:09:20.761 --> 00:09:23.695
a managed identity or a
certificate, never a secret.

140
00:09:23.695 --> 00:09:26.033
Start the job with its safety nets on:

141
00:09:26.033 --> 00:09:30.007
the notification email and
accidental-deletion prevention.

142
00:09:30.007 --> 00:09:33.283
Then send five records and
get one accepted batch.

143
00:09:33.283 --> 00:09:36.482
The step people skip is
the next one: reconcile.

144
00:09:36.482 --> 00:09:40.907
Every person key in the manifest should
show up in the provisioning logs.

145
00:09:40.907 --> 00:09:42.490
Then back it all out.

146
00:09:46.240 --> 00:09:49.915
Each of these dangers is
ordinary, and each has a guard.

147
00:09:49.915 --> 00:09:53.797
The most tempting is sending a sample's
output straight to production,

148
00:09:53.797 --> 00:09:57.277
which lets pending workers and
duplicates into the directory.

149
00:09:57.277 --> 00:10:00.880
The most damaging is one bad
extract disabling hundreds

150
00:10:00.880 --> 00:10:04.253
of people before anyone looks,
and the hold stops that.

151
00:10:04.253 --> 00:10:07.678
The quietest is treating an
accepted response as success,

152
00:10:07.678 --> 00:10:10.390
when the record may have
failed inside the job.

153
00:10:10.390 --> 00:10:14.104
Notice that every guard is something
the code does on every run,

154
00:10:14.104 --> 00:10:16.750
not something a person has to remember.

155
00:10:20.500 --> 00:10:21.850
Here's what done looks like.

156
00:10:21.850 --> 00:10:24.890
On the code side: the
sample's mapping validates,

157
00:10:24.890 --> 00:10:29.488
the pinned copy is unchanged and
reviewed, production goes through the

158
00:10:29.488 --> 00:10:33.775
hardened client, both clients pass,
and new rules refuse by default.

159
00:10:33.775 --> 00:10:38.502
On the run side: the hold and the budget
are set from your own numbers and license,

160
00:10:38.502 --> 00:10:41.120
the sender has one
permission and no secret,

161
00:10:41.120 --> 00:10:43.810
the quarantine file
stays in the audit store,

162
00:10:43.810 --> 00:10:48.537
every missing person key becomes a finding,
and the endpoint matches the cloud.

163
00:10:48.537 --> 00:10:51.999
Chapter 2 builds Graph clients
for the government clouds.

164
00:10:51.999 --> 00:10:53.160
Thanks for watching.
